Chapter: GopIcon
  cites UI chapter Icon
  cites UI chapter Vector
  cites Works chapter GopDraw

 Icons on the GOP framebuffer. `UI chapter Icon` already carries the
 artwork and the lookup; what it does not carry is a blit this path can
 afford. `icon-blit` paints into a `Framebuf`, whose `fb-set` returns a
 record per pixel at 24 bytes each, so a single 1024x768 pass costs 18.9 MB
 that nothing reclaims. The desk's buffer is linear XRGB behind `gop-put`
 at 0 bytes per pixel, and that is what these draw into.

 We say:

Section: The Kit

 Every icon in `UI chapter Icon` is a module-level definition holding a
 `List Integer`, and a module-level binding is a recipe rather than a cell:
 each mention re-materialises the whole bitmap, 64 Integers for an 8x8.
 Mentioning `icon-set-standard` inside a paint would rebuild all 63 of
 them. So the icons a surface needs are built ONCE into a kit and threaded
 as a parameter, which is the hoist the Guide prescribes for a table read
 in a loop, and no function below this section mentions an icon by name.

  IconKit = record {
    ik-dir : Icon,
    ik-src : Icon,
    ik-img : Icon,
    ik-bin : Icon
  }

  gicon-dir : Integer = 0

  gicon-src : Integer = 1

  gicon-img : Integer = 2

  gicon-bin : Integer = 3

  gicon-kit : IconKit
  gicon-kit =
    IconKit { ik-dir = ico-folder-8, ik-src = ico-code-8, ik-img = ico-image-8, ik-bin = ico-file-8 }

  gicon-pick : IconKit, Integer -> Icon
  gicon-pick (kit) (kind) =
    if kind == gicon-dir then kit.ik-dir
    else if kind == gicon-src then kit.ik-src
    else if kind == gicon-img then kit.ik-img
    else kit.ik-bin

Section: Blit

 The framebuffer is linear and `gop-put` is one `poke-32` at
 `(y * stride + x) * 4`, so a column past the end of a row lands on the
 NEXT row rather than off the screen. The bound against `stride` is the
 one `gop-draw-text` carries, for that reason and no other.

 An icon is a 1-bit mask: an on pixel takes the caller's colour and an off
 pixel is left alone, so an icon over a highlighted row keeps the row's
 fill behind it.

  gicon-cols : Integer, Integer, Icon, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-cols (base) (stride) (ico) (px) (py) (scale) (color) (row) (col) =
    if col >= ico.ico-width then 0
    else if px + (col + 1) * scale > stride then 0
    else let on = icon-get ico col row
    in let d = if on == 1 then gop-fill-rect base stride (px + col * scale) (py + row * scale) scale scale color else 0
    in gicon-cols base stride ico px py scale color row (col + 1)

  gicon-rows : Integer, Integer, Icon, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-rows (base) (stride) (ico) (px) (py) (scale) (color) (row) =
    if row >= ico.ico-height then 0
    else let r = gicon-cols base stride ico px py scale color row 0
    in gicon-rows base stride ico px py scale color (row + 1)

  gicon-blit : Integer, Integer, Icon, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-blit (base) (stride) (ico) (px) (py) (scale) (color) =
    if scale <= 0 then 0
    else gicon-rows base stride ico px py scale color 0

 The drawn size is the artwork's width times the scale it is given, and a
 caller laying a row out needs it before it draws.

  gicon-size : Icon, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-size (ico) (scale) = ico.ico-width * scale

Section: Drawn Icons

 The artwork above is a 1-bit mask eight pixels across, and `comp-icon` shows
 it at `d / 8`, so the desk's icons are eight-pixel drawings magnified three
 or four times. That is a resolution problem before it is a colour one, and
 no amount of blending fixes a shape that only has sixty-four samples in it.

 A path has no size, so it is rasterized at whatever the surface asks for.
 `vec-coverage` answers a byte of coverage per pixel; this stores that in an
 `alloc-bytes` blob rather than keeping the `List Integer`, for the reason
 the kit above exists: a module-level list is a recipe and re-materialises at
 every mention, so the artwork has to be rendered once and addressed after.

  gicon-render : VecPath, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-render (p) (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes (n * n)
    in let cov = vec-coverage-in p gicon-em n
    in let w = gicon-store buf cov 0 (n * n)
    in buf

  gicon-store : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-store (buf) (cov) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let w = poke-byte buf i (list-at cov i)
    in gicon-store buf cov (i + 1) n

  gicon-cov-at : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-cov-at (blob) (n) (x) (y) = peek-byte blob (y * n + x)

  gicon-cov-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-cov-cols (base) (stride) (blob) (n) (px) (py) (color) (row) (col) =
    if col >= n then 0
    else if px + col >= stride then 0
    else let d = gop-blend base stride (px + col) (py + row) color (gicon-cov-at blob n col row)
    in gicon-cov-cols base stride blob n px py color row (col + 1)

  gicon-cov-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-cov-rows (base) (stride) (blob) (n) (px) (py) (color) (row) =
    if row >= n then 0
    else let r = gicon-cov-cols base stride blob n px py color row 0
    in gicon-cov-rows base stride blob n px py color (row + 1)

  gicon-blit-cov : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-blit-cov (base) (stride) (blob) (n) (px) (py) (color) =
    if n <= 0 then 0
    else gicon-cov-rows base stride blob n px py color 0

 Artwork is authored in a box of `gicon-em` units and rasterized to whatever
 size a surface asks for, so one drawing serves the sidebar's gutter and the
 launcher's row without a second set.

  gicon-em : Integer = 32

 A `vec-move-to` inside a path starts a new subpath rather than a new path,
 so a drawing is as many contours as it needs. The fill pairs its scanline
 crossings, which is the even-odd rule, and that is what makes a ring out of
 two circles and a frame out of two rectangles without a second primitive:
 a contour inside another one cuts a hole. A shape meant to be solid must
 therefore not be drawn inside another one, which is why the power stem sits
 within the ring's hole rather than across its band.

  gicon-box : VecPath, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> VecPath
  gicon-box (p) (x) (y) (w) (h) =
    let a = vec-move-to p x y
    in let b = vec-line-to a (x + w) y
    in let c = vec-line-to b (x + w) (y + h)
    in let d = vec-line-to c x (y + h)
    in vec-close d

  gicon-ring : VecPath, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> VecPath
  gicon-ring (p) (cx) (cy) (ro) (ri) =
    vec-circle-loop (vec-circle-loop p cx cy ro 24 0 True) cx cy ri 24 0 True

  gicon-vec-menu : VecPath
  gicon-vec-menu =
    gicon-box (gicon-box (gicon-box vec-path-new 5 7 22 3) 5 14 22 3) 5 21 22 3

  gicon-vec-grid : VecPath
  gicon-vec-grid =
    gicon-box (gicon-box (gicon-box (gicon-box vec-path-new 5 5 9 9) 18 5 9 9) 5 18 9 9) 18 18 9 9

  gicon-vec-folder : VecPath
  gicon-vec-folder =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 4 9
    in let p2 = vec-line-to p 13 9
    in let p3 = vec-line-to p2 15 12
    in let p4 = vec-line-to p3 28 12
    in let p5 = vec-line-to p4 28 26
    in let p6 = vec-line-to p5 4 26
    in vec-close p6

  gicon-vec-edit : VecPath
  gicon-vec-edit =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 22 4
    in let p2 = vec-line-to p 28 10
    in let p3 = vec-line-to p2 13 25
    in let p4 = vec-line-to p3 5 27
    in let p5 = vec-line-to p4 7 19
    in vec-close p5

  gicon-vec-terminal : VecPath
  gicon-vec-terminal =
    let frame = gicon-box (gicon-box vec-path-new 3 6 26 20) 6 9 20 14
    in let p = vec-move-to frame 9 13
    in let p2 = vec-line-to p 15 16
    in let p3 = vec-line-to p2 9 19
    in let p4 = vec-line-to p3 9 17
    in let p5 = vec-line-to p4 12 16
    in let p6 = vec-line-to p5 9 15
    in vec-close p6

  gicon-vec-power : VecPath
  gicon-vec-power =
    gicon-box (gicon-ring vec-path-new 16 18 12 8) 14 4 4 13

 Two contours that OVERLAP cancel where they cross, because the fill pairs
 its crossings: two rectangles sharing a band leave that band empty. Nested is
 fine and is how a ring and a cut-out glyph are drawn; overlapping is not, so
 a cloud is one silhouette rather than three discs and a gear's teeth sit
 clear of its rim.

  gicon-vec-clock : VecPath
  gicon-vec-clock =
    let r = gicon-ring vec-path-new 16 16 13 10
    in gicon-box (gicon-box r 15 8 2 9) 16 15 7 2

  gicon-vec-calendar : VecPath
  gicon-vec-calendar =
    let f = gicon-box (gicon-box vec-path-new 4 7 24 21) 7 14 18 11
    in gicon-box (gicon-box f 9 3 3 6) 20 3 3 6

  gicon-vec-eye : VecPath
  gicon-vec-eye =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 3 16
    in let p2 = vec-bezier-to p 10 6 22 6 29 16
    in let p3 = vec-bezier-to p2 22 26 10 26 3 16
    in let lens = vec-close p3
    in vec-circle-loop lens 16 16 4 16 0 True

  gicon-vec-bell : VecPath
  gicon-vec-bell =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 7 22
    in let p2 = vec-bezier-to p 9 18 8 8 16 5
    in let p3 = vec-bezier-to p2 24 8 23 18 25 22
    in let p4 = vec-line-to p3 7 22
    in let body = vec-close p4
    in gicon-box body 13 24 6 3

  gicon-vec-cloud : VecPath
  gicon-vec-cloud =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 6 23
    in let p2 = vec-bezier-to p 1 23 1 15 7 15
    in let p3 = vec-bezier-to p2 7 6 19 5 21 13
    in let p4 = vec-bezier-to p3 29 12 31 23 25 23
    in vec-close p4

  gicon-vec-check : VecPath
  gicon-vec-check =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 5 16
    in let p2 = vec-line-to p 9 12
    in let p3 = vec-line-to p2 13 17
    in let p4 = vec-line-to p3 24 6
    in let p5 = vec-line-to p4 28 10
    in let p6 = vec-line-to p5 13 26
    in vec-close p6

  gicon-vec-image : VecPath
  gicon-vec-image =
    let f = gicon-box (gicon-box vec-path-new 3 6 26 20) 6 9 20 14
    in let s = vec-circle-loop f 11 14 3 16 0 True
    in let p = vec-move-to s 8 22
    in let p2 = vec-line-to p 14 15
    in let p3 = vec-line-to p2 20 22
    in vec-close p3

  gicon-vec-star : VecPath
  gicon-vec-star =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 16 3
    in let p2 = vec-line-to p 20 12
    in let p3 = vec-line-to p2 30 13
    in let p4 = vec-line-to p3 22 19
    in let p5 = vec-line-to p4 25 29
    in let p6 = vec-line-to p5 16 23
    in let p7 = vec-line-to p6 7 29
    in let p8 = vec-line-to p7 10 19
    in let p9 = vec-line-to p8 2 13
    in let p10 = vec-line-to p9 12 12
    in vec-close p10

  gicon-vec-settings : VecPath
  gicon-vec-settings =
    let r = gicon-ring vec-path-new 16 16 11 6
    in let t = gicon-box (gicon-box r 13 1 6 4) 13 27 6 4
    in gicon-box (gicon-box t 1 13 4 6) 27 13 4 6

  gicon-vec-info : VecPath
  gicon-vec-info =
    let d = vec-circle-loop vec-path-new 16 16 13 24 0 True
    in gicon-box (gicon-box d 14 8 4 4) 14 14 4 11

  gicon-vec-code : VecPath
  gicon-vec-code =
    let a = vec-move-to vec-path-new 11 8
    in let a2 = vec-line-to a 14 11
    in let a3 = vec-line-to a2 8 16
    in let a4 = vec-line-to a3 14 21
    in let a5 = vec-line-to a4 11 24
    in let a6 = vec-line-to a5 2 16
    in let left = vec-close a6
    in let b = vec-move-to left 21 8
    in let b2 = vec-line-to b 30 16
    in let b3 = vec-line-to b2 21 24
    in let b4 = vec-line-to b3 18 21
    in let b5 = vec-line-to b4 24 16
    in let b6 = vec-line-to b5 18 11
    in vec-close b6

  gicon-vec-file : VecPath
  gicon-vec-file =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 7 3
    in let p2 = vec-line-to p 19 3
    in let p3 = vec-line-to p2 25 9
    in let p4 = vec-line-to p3 25 29
    in let p5 = vec-line-to p4 7 29
    in vec-close p5

  gicon-vec-home : VecPath
  gicon-vec-home =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 16 3
    in let p2 = vec-line-to p 30 15
    in let p3 = vec-line-to p2 26 15
    in let p4 = vec-line-to p3 26 28
    in let p5 = vec-line-to p4 6 28
    in let p6 = vec-line-to p5 6 15
    in let p7 = vec-line-to p6 2 15
    in vec-close p7

  gicon-vec-named : Text -> VecPath
  gicon-vec-named (name) =
    if name == "menu" then gicon-vec-menu
    else if name == "grid" then gicon-vec-grid
    else if name == "folder" then gicon-vec-folder
    else if name == "edit" then gicon-vec-edit
    else if name == "terminal" then gicon-vec-terminal
    else if name == "power" then gicon-vec-power
    else if name == "clock" then gicon-vec-clock
    else if name == "calendar" then gicon-vec-calendar
    else if name == "eye" then gicon-vec-eye
    else if name == "bell" then gicon-vec-bell
    else if name == "cloud" then gicon-vec-cloud
    else if name == "check" then gicon-vec-check
    else if name == "image" then gicon-vec-image
    else if name == "star" then gicon-vec-star
    else if name == "settings" then gicon-vec-settings
    else if name == "info" then gicon-vec-info
    else if name == "code" then gicon-vec-code
    else if name == "home" then gicon-vec-home
    else if name == "win-close" then gicon-vec-win-close
    else if name == "win-min" then gicon-vec-win-min
    else if name == "win-max" then gicon-vec-win-max
    else gicon-vec-file

 A name with no drawing falls back to the eight pixel mask rather than to
 nothing, so the set can grow one drawing at a time and every surface keeps
 working while it does.

 THE CLOSE MARK IS ONE CONTOUR AND NOT TWO CROSSED BARS. Two bars overlap at
 the centre, and the fill pairs its scanline crossings, so the overlap would
 CANCEL and an X would come out with a hole exactly where the two strokes
 meet. Traced as a single twelve-vertex outline it never crosses itself. The
 maximise frame is the opposite case and is allowed: a box inside a box is
 NESTED, and that is how the same rule makes a ring.

  gicon-vec-win-close : VecPath
  gicon-vec-win-close =
    let a = vec-move-to vec-path-new 6 9
    in let b = vec-line-to a 9 6
    in let c = vec-line-to b 16 13
    in let d = vec-line-to c 23 6
    in let e = vec-line-to d 26 9
    in let f = vec-line-to e 19 16
    in let g = vec-line-to f 26 23
    in let i = vec-line-to g 23 26
    in let j = vec-line-to i 16 19
    in let k = vec-line-to j 9 26
    in let l = vec-line-to k 6 23
    in let m = vec-line-to l 13 16
    in vec-close m

  gicon-vec-win-min : VecPath
  gicon-vec-win-min = gicon-box vec-path-new 6 20 20 3

  gicon-vec-win-max : VecPath
  gicon-vec-win-max = gicon-box (gicon-box vec-path-new 6 6 20 20) 9 9 14 14

  gicon-drawn-count : Integer = 22

  gicon-drawn-name : Integer -> Text
  gicon-drawn-name (i) =
    if i == 0 then "menu"
    else if i == 1 then "grid"
    else if i == 2 then "folder"
    else if i == 3 then "edit"
    else if i == 4 then "terminal"
    else if i == 5 then "power"
    else if i == 6 then "clock"
    else if i == 7 then "calendar"
    else if i == 8 then "eye"
    else if i == 9 then "bell"
    else if i == 10 then "cloud"
    else if i == 11 then "check"
    else if i == 12 then "image"
    else if i == 13 then "star"
    else if i == 14 then "settings"
    else if i == 15 then "info"
    else if i == 16 then "code"
    else if i == 17 then "home"
    else if i == 19 then "win-close"
    else if i == 20 then "win-min"
    else if i == 21 then "win-max"
    else "file"

 Every name has a drawing now, and `gicon-drawn` still exists rather than
 being retired: the eight pixel masks are what a surface falls back to when a
 size disagrees with the one settled at boot, and a name added to the launcher
 tomorrow with no path of its own has to land somewhere.

  gicon-drawn-slot : Text, Integer -> Integer
  gicon-drawn-slot (name) (i) =
    if i >= gicon-drawn-count then 0 - 1
    else if gicon-drawn-name i == name then i
    else gicon-drawn-slot name (i + 1)

  gicon-drawn : Text -> Boolean
  gicon-drawn (name) = gicon-drawn-slot name 0 >= 0

  gicon-vec-disc : VecPath
  gicon-vec-disc = vec-circle 16 16 13 24

  gicon-vec-doc : VecPath
  gicon-vec-doc =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 8 4
    in let p2 = vec-line-to p 20 4
    in let p3 = vec-line-to p2 24 8
    in let p4 = vec-line-to p3 24 28
    in let p5 = vec-line-to p4 8 28
    in vec-close p5

  gicon-vec-drop : VecPath
  gicon-vec-drop =
    let p = vec-move-to vec-path-new 16 4
    in let p2 = vec-bezier-to p 28 14 28 22 16 28
    in let p3 = vec-bezier-to p2 4 22 4 14 16 4
    in vec-close p3

Section: By Name

 A surface with one icon per row cannot hold a kit: the launcher's fourteen
 entries would need fourteen fields. This answers ONE icon, so a caller pays
 for the one it draws and not for the set, and `icon-set-find` over
 `icon-set-standard` is never the road because the set mention rebuilds all
 63 bitmaps before the search starts.

 An unknown name answers the file icon rather than refusing. A missing glyph
 on a launcher row is a worse outcome than a generic one, and the caller has
 no repair to make at that point in a paint.

  gicon-named : Text -> Icon
  gicon-named (name) =
    if name == "folder" then ico-folder-8
    else if name == "code" then ico-code-8
    else if name == "image" then ico-image-8
    else if name == "clock" then ico-clock-8
    else if name == "calendar" then ico-calendar-8
    else if name == "grid" then ico-grid-8
    else if name == "eye" then ico-eye-8
    else if name == "edit" then ico-edit-8
    else if name == "bell" then ico-bell-8
    else if name == "terminal" then ico-terminal-8
    else if name == "cloud" then ico-cloud-8
    else if name == "check" then ico-check-8
    else if name == "star" then ico-star-8
    else if name == "settings" then ico-settings-8
    else if name == "info" then ico-info-8
    else if name == "menu" then ico-menu-8
    else if name == "power" then ico-power-8
    else if name == "home" then ico-home-8
    else ico-file-8
